WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:06.830
If you've ever taken the time to sit through
the full credits of a movie, you've noticed

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:11.049
that it can take thousands of people to finish
a film—which makes it that much funnier

00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:14.770
when a glaring mistake sneaks its way past
all of them.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:18.910
Here are some of the weirdest, funniest, and
all-around best movie mistakes that made the

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.590
final cut.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:21.850
Star Wars head bonk

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:26.080
Director George Lucas didn't have the biggest
budget to work with when he made the original

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:31.580
Star Wars, so he had to work with some shots
and situations that were less than ideal.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:36.320
One legendary mistake happens when a bunch
of stormtroopers burst into a Death Star control

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.780
room.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.160
One trooper, unable to see well through his
helmet, bumps his head on the door.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.190
Lucas may not have planned the shoot this
way, but when putting together his "special

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.190
edition" years later, he didn't take it out.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:52.180
Instead, he added a sound effect to acknowledge
the goof.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.559
“Take over!”

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:55.649
Elsa’s amazing hair

00:00:55.649 --> 00:00:59.170
When you're a Disney cartoon, you don't have
to obey the laws of physics.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.129
Just ask Elsa from Frozen.

00:01:01.129 --> 00:01:05.780
During her big musical number, she can flawlessly
let down her hair...and she can even send

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:10.179
her braid soaring straight through her arm
without feeling a thing.

00:01:10.179 --> 00:01:12.659
Gotham City’s horrible drivers

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:17.219
Even before Bane took over Gotham in The Dark
Knight Rises, the city had plenty of problems

00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:20.369
with criminals—but not just the superpowered
ones.

00:01:20.369 --> 00:01:24.829
When Catwoman steals Bruce Wayne's car during
one of the movie's lighter moments, it's amazing

00:01:24.829 --> 00:01:29.609
to see that literally no one on the street
is obeying the obvious red light at this intersection.

00:01:29.609 --> 00:01:32.210
Holy traffic ticket, Batman...

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.789
Captain Waterproof

00:01:33.789 --> 00:01:38.600
In Captain America: The Winter Soldier, we
see Cap diving out of a plane into the ocean

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.389
so he can stage a sneak attack on a ship—and
when he climbs out of the water, he's bone

00:01:43.389 --> 00:01:45.039
dry, shield and all.

00:01:45.039 --> 00:01:46.530
He probably learned that trick from the Sub-Mariner.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.849
Batman’s Incredible Bat-Punch

00:01:49.849 --> 00:01:54.150
It's easy to forget how much choreography
is involved in a movie fight sequence, but

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:58.100
there's a lot more to it than just pretending
to throw kicks and punches.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:03.159
One wrong move can mean the difference between
a scene that looks convincing, and one that…well,

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:06.289
looks like the rooftop fight from The Dark
Knight Rises.

00:02:06.289 --> 00:02:10.700
There's a lot going on here, including a fateful
turn in the relationship between Batman and

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:11.780
Catwoman.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:16.310
So most of us didn't notice that one of Bane's
henchmen takes a dive during the fight, falling

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:20.040
down without anyone even laying a hand on
him.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.750
The Amazing Spider-Lamp

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.590
The best part of having multiple Spider-Man
reboots is getting to see Spidey learning

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.420
how to use his powers all over again, right?

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:29.420
Just kidding!

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.180
It's the most boring part of the story.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:36.810
But in 2002's Spider-Man, our young hero's
training footage comes with a memorable blooper:

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.640
he breaks a lamp while testing his webbing,
but when Aunt May comes knocking at the door

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.390
moments later, it's back on the shelf like
nothing ever happened.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:48.440
One possible explanation: it was bitten by
a radioactive lampshade.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.900
Another possible explanation: lazy editors.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.650
Harry Potter and the Visible Cameraman

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:58.870
In one scene from Harry Potter and the Chamber
of Secrets, a tense battle of wands between

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:04.030
Harry and the villainous Draco Malfoy includes
an extra bit of magic...in the form of the

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.480
Muggle cameraman who can be glimpsed filming
after Harry knocks Draco down.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.970
Maybe he's there shooting an MTV reality series...Sixteen
and Magic.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:15.850
The mysterious hedge maze

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:20.940
Stanley Kubrick's The Shining is a masterpiece
that uses disorientation to create a growing

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.100
feeling of unease in the viewer.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.880
As Shining experts have pointed out over the
years, the movie's setting—the remote Overlook

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.000
Hotel—has a floor plan that's riddled with
spatial impossibilities.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.640
The weirdness doesn't stop at the hotel walls,
either: the movie's climax makes brilliant

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.100
use of a creepy hedge maze—which is nowhere
to be seen during the sweeping aerial shot

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.430
of the Overlook that opens the movie.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.610
Unintentional goof, or twisted Kubrickian
genius?

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:47.910
You decide.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.130
The little anticipator

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:53.900
One of the greatest goofs in classic cinema
occurs during this scene from the vintage

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.660
Hitchcock thriller North by Northwest.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:01.400
Watch the background during Cary Grant's argument
with Eva Marie Saint, specifically when Saint's

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.130
character pulls a gun on Grant.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:07.520
A child actor, who'd evidently gotten a case
of nerves after reading the script or suffering

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.770
through a few too many takes, covers his ears
well before his character is supposed to be

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.490
aware of what's going on.

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:16.209
Teen Wolf flasher

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:21.039
There's no shortage of cheesy silliness in
the mid-'80s Michael J. Fox cult comedy classic

00:04:21.039 --> 00:04:22.039
Teen Wolf.

00:04:22.039 --> 00:04:27.419
After all, it's about a suburban kid who suddenly
realizes he's, you know, a teen werewolf.

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:29.300
Not much subtlety in that title.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:33.919
But there's a background extra in the movie's
final act who actually is pretty subtle, and

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:36.779
who may give you a quick laugh if you're watching
closely.

00:04:36.779 --> 00:04:40.919
The moment in question occurs during the big
celebration after Fox's character wins the

00:04:40.919 --> 00:04:45.949
big game, and a celebration erupts in the
stands—evidently surprising one young woman

00:04:45.949 --> 00:04:50.490
in the crowd, who can be seen cheering with
her pants completely unzipped and unbuttoned.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:53.270
She manages to get them closed up later in
the scene.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:57.889
But, really, let's take a moment to recognize
that a nameless extra managed to become more

00:04:57.889 --> 00:05:03.490
interesting than the werewolf who just won
a high school basketball game.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:07.439
Give that woman an Oscar.

00:05:07.439 --> 00:05:09.460
The Commando Porsche

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:14.040
There are so many questions in Arnold Schwarzenegger's
1980s action masterpiece Commando.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.930
Like, why is this big Austrian's name John
Matrix?

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:20.129
And how is Alyssa Milano his daughter?

00:05:20.129 --> 00:05:23.090
And more than anything, what's the deal with
his magic yellow Porsche?

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:27.210
We're talking, of course, about the part of
the movie that sees Matrix chasing down the

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.719
bad guy, Sully, trashing the Porsche in the
process.

00:05:30.719 --> 00:05:34.909
The car seems destined for a few weeks in
a body shop…until John gets back behind

00:05:34.909 --> 00:05:38.570
the wheel, at which point it appears miraculously
damage-free.

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:39.870
Thanks for watching!

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:43.490
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to see more
videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:47.440
And leave us a comment to tell us your favorite
movie mistakes that should've made the list!

